  <h1>About WrongfulTerminationCalc</h1>

  <h2>Our Mission</h2>
  <p>
    WrongfulTerminationCalc exists to close an information gap that affects millions of American workers every year. When someone is fired, the experience is disorienting, financially frightening, and legally complex. Most people don't know if their termination was illegal, what damages they might recover, or what steps to take first.
  </p>
  <p>
    Employment attorneys — the people who know the answers — are often inaccessible. Consultations cost money. Free legal aid is overextended. And the internet is full of generic, unreviewed content that confuses more than it helps.
  </p>
  <p>
    We built WrongfulTerminationCalc to give workers a credible, editorially rigorous starting point. Our calculator, guides, and articles are written and reviewed by our editorial team before publication. We clearly label what is a legal estimate and what is established law. We do not offer legal advice — but we do offer the kind of informed, accurate information that helps workers understand whether they should be talking to an attorney.
  </p>

  <h2>What We Provide</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A free wrongful termination settlement calculator that estimates damages ranges based on claim type, salary, tenure, and contract status</li>
    <li>Plain-language explanations of the main types of wrongful termination claims</li>
    <li>Practical guides on what to do after a wrongful termination and how to document your case</li>
    <li>Methodology transparency: we explain exactly how our calculator works and where its estimates come from</li>
    <li>Editorial oversight: all legal content is reviewed by our editorial team</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who We Are</h2>
  <p>
    WrongfulTerminationCalc is published by The Click Lab, a digital media company that builds informational tools for consumers navigating legal and financial decisions. Our editorial team brings employment-law and HR-compliance expertise to every page. See our <a href="/editorial/">editorial team page</a> for more on our review process.
  </p>

  <h2>Legal Disclaimer</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The information on this website is for educational and informational purposes only. It does not constitute legal advice and does not create an attorney-client relationship.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Employment law is highly fact-specific and varies significantly by state and jurisdiction. The calculator results on this site are estimates based on publicly available data about typical case outcomes — they are not predictions of what your case will produce. Many factors that are critical to the actual value of a wrongful termination claim cannot be captured by an online calculator, including the quality of your evidence, the credibility of witnesses, the financial resources of your employer, and the specific legal standards applied in your jurisdiction.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you believe you have been wrongfully terminated, you should consult a licensed employment attorney in your state as soon as possible. Many employment law violations have short statutes of limitations — as short as 180 days for EEOC charge filing in some states — and missing these deadlines can permanently bar your claims.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing on this site should be relied upon as a substitute for professional legal advice from an attorney who has reviewed the specific facts of your situation.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact Us</h2>
  <p>
    For editorial inquiries, corrections, or general questions, reach us at <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We do not respond to requests for legal advice.
  </p>

